+# ── Listen address (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
+# Unset (the default) listens on every interface, which is what the bundled
+# compose stack wants — the API is only reachable on the internal network. If
+# you run the API directly on a host instead, bind it to loopback so the only
+# way in is the web server in front of it:
+#
+#   HOST=127.0.0.1
+
+# ── Single sign-on / OpenID Connect (optional) ─────────────────────────────
+# Off unless all three of the first settings are given. When on, a "Sign in
+# with …" button appears next to the passkey one and both work: SSO does not
+# replace passkeys, it joins them. Every route behind either is identical.
+#
+#   OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com/application/o/opengym/
+#   OIDC_CLIENT_ID=opengym
+#   OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=…
+#
+# The discovery document is read from <issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration.
+# Register this exact redirect URI with your provider — it is derived from
+# ORIGIN and cannot be set separately, because it has to be same-origin with
+# the app just like passkeys are:
+#
+#   <ORIGIN>/api/oidc/callback
+#
+# Authorization-code flow with PKCE. Confidential client, so the provider must
+# be set to require a client secret.
+#
+# Label on the button. Cosmetic:
+#
+#   OIDC_NAME=SSO
+#
+# Scopes requested. The default is almost always right; `profile` is also what
+# carries group membership on most providers:
+#
+#   OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email
+#
+# Groups from the id_token's `groups` claim that get the admin dashboard.
+# Empty (default) leaves admin to ADMIN_UIDS. Set it and it becomes
+# authoritative for SSO users, re-checked at every sign-in — so removing
+# somebody from the group in your provider actually takes their admin away:
+#
+#   OIDC_ADMIN_GROUPS=service_admins,household_admins
+#
+# Create a profile the first time somebody your provider vouches for signs in.
+# On by default. INVITE_ONLY does not apply here: it gates the open passkey
+# signup form, while everyone arriving through SSO has already been vetted by
+# your provider. Turn it off to allow SSO only into profiles that exist:
+#
+#   OIDC_AUTO_PROVISION=0
+#
+# Adopt an existing passkey profile whose name matches, rather than making a
+# second one next to it. On by default, and it is what makes an instance that
+# already has people on it migratable — without it everyone's history appears
+# to vanish at their first SSO login. Turn it off if two people on this
+# instance share a display name:
+#
+#   OIDC_LINK_EXISTING=0
+#
+# SSO does not close the other doors. To make it the only way in, close them:
+# INVITE_ONLY=1 (no new passkey profiles) and ALLOW_GUEST=0 (no local-only
+# guests). Existing passkeys keep working — remove them from ./data/db.json if
+# that is not what you want.
+
